@lang en
-- UNOFFICIAL TEXT. This is a plain-text reproduction, made by legalese commons, of the consolidation published by the King's Printer, Province of British Columbia (BC Laws). It is not an official version.
-- Source: https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/96238_01/xml (BC Laws consolidation, current to 2026-10-13).
-- These materials contain information that has been derived from information originally made available by the Province of British Columbia at: http://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca and this information is being used in accordance with the King's Printer Licence – British Columbia available at: https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/standards/Licence.html. They have not, however, been produced in affiliation with, or with the endorsement of, the Province of British Columbia and THESE MATERIALS ARE NOT AN OFFICIAL VERSION.
--
-- The Interpretation Act (British Columbia), RSBC 1996, c. 238: the rules that count time.
-- A reusable module: it imports only `prelude` and `daydate`, so that an encoding of any
-- other British Columbia enactment can IMPORT it and ask when a period ends, when an
-- enactment binds, and what a holiday does to a deadline.
--
-- Draft. A single Sonnet 5.5 agent wrote this from the text alone; no domain expert has read it
-- against the statute. The quotations are copied from `RSBC-1996-c.-238.txt`, a plain-text
-- rendering made by a script (not the official text), by `srcrefs.py`; `src:N` is line N of that file.
--
-- Four conventions hold throughout.
--   * Days of rest, recess and holiday are never computed: the caller supplies them as a
--     function from a day to TRUE or FALSE (s 25(3), s 25(4), s 25.5, s 29 "holiday").
--     That function is the WHOLE calendar: nothing tops it up with Sundays, and a calendar that
--     leaves them out silently gives wrong answers. To get the Sundays and the 26 December that
--     the text itself names, build it as `s 29 a day is a holiday` applied to your other holidays.
--   * Where the text does not answer, the answer is a named REFUSE, never a default.
--   * A period is counted as the text counts it: the reference day is NOT in a period that
--     runs from or after it (s 25.2(2)(a)), and the last day of a period is inside it.
--   * Target compiler: `unstable` (REFUSE and YMD are rejected by `main`).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `Types`

-- The reference day (s 25(1)) may lie before the period (a period "from" or "after" it) or
-- after the period (a period "before" it). The text names both limbs in every counting rule.
DECLARE `Position of the reference day` IS ONE OF
    `The reference day is before the period`
    `The reference day is after the period`

DECLARE `Unit of a period` IS ONE OF
    Days
    Weeks
    Months
    Years

-- `clear` is TRUE when the period is "expressed as" "clear" days, weeks, months or years,
-- or as "at least" or "not less than" a number of them (ss 25.2(3), 25.3(2), 25.4(2)).
DECLARE `Period of time` HAS
    `unit` IS A `Unit of a period`
    `how many` IS A NUMBER
    `clear` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Span of days` HAS
    `first day` IS A DATE
    `last day` IS A DATE

-- A moment in British Columbia, as the text means it (s 26(2)): Pacific Time, which the text
-- fixes at 7 hours behind UTC all year round (s 26(1)).
DECLARE `Pacific Time moment` HAS
    `calendar day` IS A DATE
    `hour of the day` IS A NUMBER
    `minute of the hour` IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `UTC moment` HAS
    `calendar day` IS A DATE
    `hour of the day` IS A NUMBER
    `minute of the hour` IS A NUMBER

§ `Named refusals`

-- One definition per refusal; the definition's name is its message (writing-l4-rules, entry 11.5).
-- Every rule that needs a refusal returns the name, so that nothing downstream can default it.

-- src:173 | (2) Subject to subsection (3), the beginning or end of a period of one day or consecutive days or of one week or consecutive weeks, expressed in relation to a reference day, is to be determined as follows:
-- src:181 | 25.3 (1) Subject to subsection (2), the beginning or end of a period of one month or consecutive months, expressed in relation to a reference day, is to be determined as follows:
-- src:190 | 25.4 (1) Subject to subsection (2), the beginning or end of a period of one year or consecutive years, expressed in relation to a reference day, is to be determined as follows:
@ref Interpretation Act (BC) s 25.2(2), s 25.3(1), s 25.4(1) (src:173, src:181, src:190)
GIVETH A DATE
`ss 25.2 to 25.4 count a whole number of one or more days, weeks, months or years` MEANS
    REFUSE "ss 25.2 to 25.4 count a whole number of one or more days, weeks, months or years"

-- src:181 | 25.3 (1) Subject to subsection (2), the beginning or end of a period of one month or consecutive months, expressed in relation to a reference day, is to be determined as follows:
-- src:182 | (a) if the reference day is before the period, by going forward to and including the date numerically corresponding to the reference day in the calendar month in which the period begins or ends, as the case may be;
-- src:183 | (b) if the reference day is after the period, by going backward to and including the date numerically corresponding to the reference day in the calendar month in which the period begins or ends, as the case may be.
-- src:184 | (2) If a period described in subsection (1) is expressed as "clear" months or "at least" or "not less than" a number of months,
-- src:185 | (a) the beginning or end of the period, as determined under subsection (1) (a), is one day later, and
-- src:186 | (b) the beginning or end of the period, as determined under subsection (1) (b), is one day earlier.
-- src:187 | (3) If the beginning or end of a period determined under subsection (1) or subsections (1) and (2), as applicable, would fall on a date that does not occur in the calendar month in which the period begins or ends, the period begins or ends, as the case may be, on the last day of that month.
@ref Interpretation Act (BC) s 25.3(2) and (3) (src:181-187)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 25.3 does not say how to shift a clear month by a day when the day numerically corresponding to the reference day does not occur` MEANS
    REFUSE "s 25.3 does not say how to shift a clear month by a day when the day numerically corresponding to the reference day does not occur"

-- src:190 | 25.4 (1) Subject to subsection (2), the beginning or end of a period of one year or consecutive years, expressed in relation to a reference day, is to be determined as follows:
-- src:191 | (a) if the reference day is before the period, by going forward to and including the date numerically corresponding to the reference day in the same calendar month as the reference day in the year in which the period begins or ends, as the case may be;
-- src:192 | (b) if the reference day is after the period, by going backward to and including the date numerically corresponding to the reference day in the same calendar month as the reference day in the year in which the period begins or ends, as the case may be.
-- src:193 | (2) If a period described in subsection (1) is expressed as "clear" years or "at least" or "not less than" a number of years,
-- src:194 | (a) the beginning or end of the period, as determined under subsection (1) (a), is one day later, and
-- src:195 | (b) the beginning or end of the period, as determined under subsection (1) (b), is one day earlier.
-- src:196 | (3) If the beginning or end of a period determined under subsection (1) or subsections (1) and (2), as applicable, would be February 29 and February 29 does not occur in the year in which the period begins or ends, the period begins or ends, as the case may be, on February 28.
@ref Interpretation Act (BC) s 25.4(2) and (3) (src:190-196)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 25.4 does not say how to shift a clear year by a day when the reference day is 29 February and the year has none` MEANS
    REFUSE "s 25.4 does not say how to shift a clear year by a day when the reference day is 29 February and the year has none"

-- src:159 | (3) A period for doing an act that is determined under section 25.2, 25.3 or 25.4 to begin or end on a holiday begins or ends, as the case may be,
-- src:160 | (a) if the reference day is before the period, on the next day that is not a holiday, and
-- src:161 | (b) if the reference day is after the period, on the previous day that is not a holiday.
@ref Interpretation Act (BC) s 25(3) (src:159-161)
GIVETH A DATE
`the holiday calendar supplied has no day free of holidays within a year` MEANS
